        "<strong>B’midbar (<em>בְּמִדְבַּר</em>) — “in the wilderness.”</strong> The Hebrew name of this book is taken from its fifth word — <em>b’midbar Sinai</em>, “in the wilderness of Sinai.” The English title <em>Numbers</em> follows the LXX <em>Arithmoi</em>, named for the two censuses (chs 1 and 26) that bracket the book. Both titles capture something true: <em>Numbers</em> the bookkeeping, <em>B’midbar</em> the formative space. The wilderness is not just setting but theology — the place where Israel is forged into a people through forty years of testing.",
