        "<strong>The fighting-age census.</strong> The number returned (603,550 in 1:46) is staggering — implying a total population, with women and children, well over two million. Most modern scholars read the figure non-literally: as schematic, symbolic of the twelve-tribe ideal, or as a corruption of <em>’elef</em> (“thousand”) for an older meaning of “military unit” or “clan.” Under the latter reading, the count would be ~600 units totaling ~5,500 men, far more plausible for a Late Bronze Age semi-nomadic group.",
