    (
        6,
        24,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Numbers</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>Ephrem reads the threefold Aaronic blessing (Num 6:24-26 — &#x27;The Lord bless thee... make his face shine upon thee... lift up his countenance upon thee&#x27;) as the canonical priestly anchor that the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit may be invoked under three benedictions before the Tabernacle reveals the Trinity. The triadic shape, the divine-face theology, and the placement of the Name upon Israel are read by Ephrem as the seed-form of every later Christian benediction. The Tewahedo Mass (Qǝddase) preserves Num 6:24-26 as the canonical dismissal-blessing; the priest extends the blessing over the people in the exact threefold form, sealing the eucharistic act.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Numbers, on 6:22-27. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD.",
    ),
