    (
        11,
        4,
        "",
        "mixed multitude",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Asafsuf (<em>אֲסַפְסוּף</em>).</strong> A reduplicated, almost onomatopoeic word — “rabble, riff-raff, motley crew.” Distinct from <em>‘erev rav</em> (Exod 12:38), the more neutral “mixed multitude” of non-Israelites who came up from Egypt with Israel. Here Numbers turns the phrase pejorative: the foreign element is identified as the locus of culinary nostalgia for Egypt — fish, cucumbers, melons, leeks, onions, garlic (11:5). The complaint is so specific it sounds like genuine memory.",
        "User original",
    ),
