    # ---- ch 12 ----
    (
        12,
        1,
        "",
        "Cushite woman",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Two readings of the Cushite wife.</strong> Cush in the Hebrew Bible designates the region south of Egypt — modern Sudan / Ethiopia. Two main interpretive traditions: (1) The Cushite woman is Zipporah, Moses’ Midianite wife, with “Cushite” a poetic variant. (2) She is a second wife, distinct from Zipporah. The early rabbinic and Patristic readings split between these. In the Ethiopian Christian tradition this verse has been read as scriptural recognition of African presence at Israel’s origins — and Miriam’s leprous punishment (12:10) as divine rebuke of color prejudice. The text itself does not specify the cause of the offense; it states only that Miriam and Aaron <em>spoke against</em> Moses.",
        "User original",
    ),
