    (
        13,
        33,
        "",
        "Nephilim",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Nephilim (<em>נְפִילִים</em>) — “the fallen.”</strong> The same word as Genesis 6:4. The spies’ report is the second and last appearance in the Bible; afterward they vanish from the text. Whether the spies actually saw giants or invented them out of fear is left open. The framing — “we were in our own sight as grasshoppers, and so we were in their sight” — is a remarkable piece of psychological observation: how the perception of an enemy reflects the perception of oneself. The Anakim (Anakites) of v. 33b are a more historical-seeming people, mentioned in the Egyptian Execration Texts (~1900 BCE).",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        13,
        33,
        "a",
        "",
        "topic-nave",
        "Topic",
        "Topic.",
        "<strong>Topics.</strong> This verse appears under: ANAKIM, COWARDICE, GRASSHOPPER, MANASSEH, NAPHTALI, REUBEN, SIMEON, ZEBULUN, MAJORITY AND MINORITY REPORTS.",
        "Nave's Topical Bible, Orville J. Nave (1896). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        13,
        33,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Numbers</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On the spies&#x27; report of &#x27;the sons of Anak, who come of the Nephilim&#x27; in Canaan (Num 13:33), Ephrem reads the survival of Nephilim-stock beyond the Flood as the canonical anchor of the continuing struggle against Watchers-progeny: not all giants perished in the deluge, and the conquest of Canaan is itself a continuation of the Flood-judgment. Tewahedo angelology, drawing on 1 Enoch 7-9 (Mäṣḥafä Hēnok), preserves this reading; the Anakim are the residual giants whose presence in Canaan justifies the herem-conquest as cosmic purgation, not merely political dispossession.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Numbers, on 13:25-33. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. 1 Enoch 7-9, trans. R.H. Charles (Oxford: Clarendon, 1912 — PD).",
    ),
