    (
        14,
        18,
        "",
        "slow to anger, and abundant in loving kindness",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>The Thirteen Attributes formula (or its echo).</strong> A truncated version of God’s self-revelation to Moses at Sinai (Exod 34:6–7). In Jewish tradition this expanded formula is the climactic prayer of Yom Kippur and the High Holy Days. Here Moses cites it back to God as a legal argument — “you said this about yourself, now act on it.” The juxtaposition is striking: the sentence begins in mercy (slow to anger, abundant in loving-kindness) and ends in judgment (visiting iniquity to the third and fourth generation). Both are God; the prayer asks which one will speak today.",
        "User original",
    ),
