    (
        15,
        38,
        "",
        "fringes on the borders of their garments",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Tzitzit (<em>צִיצִת</em>) — “fringes, tassels.”</strong> Worn at the four corners of the garment, with a thread of <em>t’chelet</em> (sky-blue) according to v. 38. The blue thread fell out of use after the Roman period (when the murex-snail dye source was lost) and was substituted with white; recent rabbinic decisors have permitted reintroducing dyed thread now that the species has been re-identified. The tzitzit is one of the most visible and persistent commandments in Jewish observance — the tassel <em>seen</em> (15:39) prompting remembrance. The garment edge of Jesus that the bleeding woman touches in Mark 5:27 is almost certainly his tzitzit.",
        "User original",
    ),
