    # ---- ch 16 ----
    (
        16,
        1,
        "",
        "Korah, the son of Izhar",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Korah’s rebellion.</strong> The most serious challenge to Moses’ authority in the Pentateuch. Korah is a Levite — a kinsman of Moses and Aaron — who argues for democratized priesthood: <em>all the congregation are holy, every one of them</em> (16:3). The text portrays this not as a theological debate but as a coup; the earth opens and swallows the rebels (16:31–33). Yet Korah’s sons survive (26:11) and their descendants compose the Korahite Psalms (Pss 42, 44–49, 84–85, 87–88) — a memorable rebuke to the idea that biblical narrative draws clean lines between heroes and villains.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        16,
        1,
        "a",
        "",
        "topic-nave",
        "Topic",
        "Topic.",
        "<strong>Topics.</strong> This verse appears under: DATHAN, ELIAB, MANASSEH, NAPHTALI, REUBEN, SIMEON, ZEBULUN, ON, PELETH.",
        "Nave's Topical Bible, Orville J. Nave (1896). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        16,
        1,
        "b",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>ABIRAM</strong> father of height; i.e., “proud.” (1.) One of the sons of Eliab, who joined Korah in the conspiracy against Moses and Aaron. He and all the conspirators, with their families and possessions (except the children of Korah), were swallowed up by an earthquake (Numbers 16:1-27; 26:9; Psalm 106:17). (2.) The eldest son of Hiel the Bethelite, who perished prematurely in consequence of his father’s undertaking to rebuild Jericho (1 Kings 16:34), according to the words of Joshua (6:26). (See JERICHO.)",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        16,
        1,
        "c",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>DATHAN</strong> welled; belonging to a fountain, a son of Eliab, a Reubenite, who joined Korah (q.v.) in his conspiracy, and with his accomplices was swallowed up by an earthquake (Numbers 16:1; 26:9; Deuteronomy 11:6; Psalm 106:17).",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        16,
        1,
        "d",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>ELIAB</strong> to whom God is father. (1.) A Reubenite, son of Pallu (Numbers 16:1, 12; 26:8, 9; Deuteronomy 11:6). (2.) A son of Helon, and chief of the tribe of Zebulun at the time of the census in the wilderness (Numbers 1:9; 2:7). (3.) The son of Jesse, and brother of David (1 Samuel 16:6). It was he who spoke contemptuously to David when he proposed to fight Goliath (1 Samuel 17:28). (4.) One of the Gadite heroes who joined David in his stronghold in the wilderness (1 Chronicles 12:9).",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        16,
        1,
        "e",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>PELETH</strong> swiftness. (1.) A Reubenite whose son was one of the conspirators against Moses and Aaron (Numbers 16:1). (2.) One of the sons of Jonathan (1 Chronicles 2:33).",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
