    # ---- ch 19 ----
    (
        19,
        2,
        "",
        "red heifer",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Parah adumah (<em>פָּרָה</em> <em>אֲדֻמָּה</em>).</strong> One of the most enigmatic rituals in the Torah. A perfect red cow, never yoked, is slaughtered outside the camp and burned whole; its ashes are mixed with water and used to purify those defiled by corpse-contact. Strikingly, the priest who prepares the ashes becomes ritually impure himself — the rite cleanses some while defiling others. The classic rabbinic comment (<em>Bemidbar Rabbah</em> 19:1) is that even Solomon could not understand it. The red heifer is one of the few rites for which the absence of the Temple has practical messianic stakes in some streams of contemporary Judaism — its restoration is considered a precondition for resumed Temple service.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        19,
        2,
        "a",
        "LORD",
        "lang-hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew.",
        "<strong>ʼădônây (<em>אֲדֹנָי</em>).</strong> the Lord (used as a proper name of God only).",
        "Strong's H136, A Concise Dictionary of the Words in the Hebrew Bible, James Strong (1894). PD.",
    ),
