    (
        21,
        8,
        "",
        "Make a  venomous snake",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Saraph (<em>שָׂרָף</em>) — “fiery one.”</strong> The same root as the seraphim of Isaiah 6. The snake is described both as <em>nachash</em> (the generic word) and <em>saraph</em> (a fiery serpent — possibly referring to a venom that burns). The bronze serpent itself, called <em>Nehushtan</em>, is preserved in Israel for centuries until King Hezekiah destroys it because the people had begun burning incense to it (2 Kings 18:4) — a remarkable example of biblical self-correction, a sacred relic redefined as idol. Jesus invokes the same bronze serpent in John 3:14 as a type of his own crucifixion.",
        "User original",
    ),
