    (
        23,
        19,
        "",
        "God is not a man",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The first oracle.</strong> Balaam’s second oracle (23:18–24) contains some of the most theologically dense lines in the Pentateuch. The denial of divine duplicity is paradoxical given that elsewhere God is said to “relent” or “change his mind” (Gen 6:6, Exod 32:14, 1 Sam 15:11) — passages which use precisely the verb (<em>nacham</em>) denied here. The tension is theological, not contradiction: God’s commitment to his stated word is unchanging; his stance toward those who act differently in time may shift. Both Numbers 23 and 1 Samuel 15:29 sit alongside the relenting passages without resolution; the contradiction is felt to be productive rather than embarrassing.",
        "User original",
    ),
