    (
        24,
        17,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Numbers</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On Balaam&#x27;s fourth oracle — &#x27;I shall see him, but not now: I shall behold him, but not nigh: there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Israel&#x27; (Num 24:17) — Ephrem reads the doubled-image (Star + Sceptre) as the canonical Old-Testament prophecy of the Messiah&#x27;s advent: the Star is the sign that appears to the Gentile Magi (Mt 2:2 — &#x27;we have seen his star in the east&#x27;), the Sceptre is the Davidic kingship received by the Son of David. The Gentile diviner Balaam prophesies through gritted teeth the salvation of the world. Tewahedo nativity-iconography cites Num 24:17 as the canonical Old-Testament doubled-anchor of the Star-of-Bethlehem and the Davidic-Solomonic Sceptre — both inherited by the Tewahedo Solomonic dynasty&#x27;s self-understanding.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Numbers, on 24:15-19. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Mt 2:2.",
    ),
