    (
        25,
        11,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Numbers</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On Phinehas&#x27;s spear-thrust and the Lord&#x27;s grant of &#x27;my covenant of peace&#x27; (Num 25:11-13), Ephrem reads the zealot-priest&#x27;s act as the canonical anchor of priestly-zeal: not bloodlust, but a refusal to tolerate the pollution of the camp by Baal of Peor. The covenant-of-peace given for an act of violence is the paradox: peace with God is procured by hating what God hates. Tewahedo priestly tradition cites Num 25:11 as the canonical Old-Testament warrant for the priestly-zeal expected of the qes (parish priest) and the märigeta (cantor) — those who cannot tolerate liturgical pollution receive the covenant of peace. The doubling with Jub 30:18 (Levi&#x27;s priesthood earned-by-zeal at Shechem) makes Num 25:11 + Jub 30:18 the canonical Tewahedo doubled-warrant for priesthood-by-zeal.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Numbers, on 25:1-15. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Jub 30:18 (R.H. Charles trans., 1902 — PD).",
    ),
    (
        25,
        11,
        "b",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>ZEAL</strong> an earnest temper; may be enlightened (Numbers 25:11-13; 2 Corinthians 7:11; 9:2), or ignorant and misdirected (Romans 10:2; Phil. 3:6). As a Christian grace, it must be grounded on right principles and directed to right ends (Galatians 4:18). It is sometimes ascribed to God (2 Kings 19:31; Isaiah 9:7; 37:32; Ezekiel 5:13).",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
