    # ---- ch 27 ----
    (
        27,
        1,
        "",
        "daughters of Zelophehad",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Zelophehad case.</strong> A rare instance of a legal innovation prompted by a specific human petition. Five daughters — Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, and Tirzah — argue that their father died without sons, and his name should not vanish from his clan. Moses brings the case to God; the answer reverses the default exclusion of daughters from inheritance (27:7–8). The case is reopened in chapter 36 with a restriction (they must marry within the tribe, lest land transfer between tribes), but the precedent stands: Israelite law is shown to be capable of revision in response to pressure from below, and from women specifically.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        27,
        1,
        "a",
        "",
        "topic-nave",
        "Topic",
        "Topic.",
        "<strong>Topics.</strong> This verse appears under: DAUGHTER, GILEAD, HEPHER, HOGLAH, MANASSEH, NAPHTALI, REUBEN, SIMEON, ZEBULUN.",
        "Nave's Topical Bible, Orville J. Nave (1896). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        27,
        1,
        "b",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>MAHLAH</strong> disease, one of the five daughters of Zelophehad (Numbers 27:1-11) who had their father’s inheritance, the law of inheritance having been altered in their favour.",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        27,
        1,
        "c",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>ZELOPHEHAD</strong> first-born, of the tribe of Manasseh, and of the family of Gilead; died in the wilderness. Having left no sons, his daughters, concerned lest their father’s name should be “done away from among his family,” made an appeal to Moses, who, by divine direction, appointed it as “a statute of judgment” in Israel that daughters should inherit their father’s portion when no sons were left (Numbers 27:1-11). But that the possession of Zelophehad might not pass away in the year of jubilee from the tribe to which he belonged, it was ordained by Moses that his daughters should not marry any one out of their father’s tribe; and this afterwards became a general law (Numbers 36).",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
