    (
        28,
        1,
        "a",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>FESTIVALS</strong> , RELIGIOUS There were daily (Leviticus 23), weekly, monthly, and yearly festivals, and great stress was laid on the regular observance of them in every particular (Numbers 28:1-8; Exodus 29:38-42; Leviticus 6:8-23; Exodus 30:7-9; 27:20). (1.) The septenary festivals were, (a) The weekly Sabbath (Leviticus 23:1-3; Exodus 19:3-30; 20:8-11; 31:12, etc.). (b) The seventh new moon, or the feast of Trumpets (Numbers 28:11-15; 29:1-6). (c) The Sabbatical year (Exodus 23:10, 11; Leviticus 25:2-7). (d) The year of jubilee (Leviticus 23-35; 25: 8-16; 27:16-25). (2.) The great feasts were, (a) The Passover. (b) The feast of Pentecost, or of weeks. (c) The feast of Tabernacles, or of ingathering. On each of these occasions every male Israelite was commanded “to appear before the Lord” (Deuteronomy 27:7; Nehemiah 8:9-12). The attendance of women was voluntary. (Comp. Luke 2:41; 1 Samuel 1:7; 2:19.) The promise that God would protect their homes (Exodus 34:23, 24) while all the males were absent in Jerusalem at these feasts was always fulfilled. “During the whole period between Moses and Christ we never read of an enemy invading the land at the time of the three festivals. The first instance on record is thirty-three years after they had withdrawn from themselves the divine protection by imbruing their hands in the Saviour’s blood, when Cestius, the Roman general, slew fifty of the people of Lydda while all the rest had gone up to the feast of Tabernacles, A.D. 66.” These festivals, besides their religious purpose, had an important bearing on the maintenance among the people of the feeling of a national unity. The times fixed for their observance were arranged so as to interfere as little as possible with the industry of the people. The Passover was kept just before the harvest commenced, Pentecost at the conclusion of the corn harvest and before the vintage, the feast of Tabernacles after all the fruits of the ground had been gathered in. (3.) The Day of Atonement, the tenth day of the seventh month (Leviticus 16:1, 34; 23:26-32; Numbers 29:7-11). (See ATONEMENT, DAY OF.) Of the post-Exilian festivals reference is made to the feast of Dedication (John 10:22). This feast was appointed by Judas Maccabaeus in commemoration of the purification of the temple after it had been polluted by Antiochus Epiphanes. The “feast of Purim” (q.v.), Esther 9:24-32, was also instituted after the Exile. (Cf. John 5:1.)",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
    # ---- ch 28 ----
    (
        28,
        2,
        "",
        "in their due season",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>B’mo‘ado (<em>בְּמוֹעֲדוֹ</em>).</strong> “At its appointed time.” The same root as <em>mo‘edim</em>, the appointed festival times of Lev 23. Numbers 28–29 lays out the fullest Pentateuchal calendar of public sacrifices — daily, Sabbath, monthly (new moon), and the annual festivals. Synagogue liturgy preserves the structure: the Torah readings on each festival come from these chapters. Christian liturgical calendars, including the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo cycle, ultimately descend from this calendar by way of the early Jewish-Christian community’s adaptation.",
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- expansion (May 2026) ----
    (
        2,
        2,
        "",
        "every man by his own standard",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The camp arrangement.</strong> The camp is described as a square with the Tent of Meeting at its center, three tribes on each of the four sides — east (Judah), south (Reuben), west (Ephraim), north (Dan). The arrangement was read by later mystics as a cosmological diagram: the sanctuary at the center mirrors the divine throne, with Israel arrayed around it like the heavenly host. The Qumran <em>War Scroll</em> reorganizes its eschatological army on exactly this Numbers-2 pattern.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        4,
        3,
        "",
        "from thirty years old and upward even until fifty years old",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Levitical service ages.</strong> The age range here (30–50) differs from Num 8:24–25 (25–50) and 1 Chr 23:24–27 (20–50). The discrepancies likely reflect different historical periods of cultic practice — earlier and later traditions about how young Levites entered active service. The Mishnah preserves yet another tradition. The Bible’s own multiplicity here is a useful reminder that priestly procedure evolved over centuries.",
        "User paraphrase; references Mishnah",
    ),
    (
        8,
        4,
        "",
        "beaten work of gold",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The lamp stand.</strong> The <em>menorah</em>, made of a single hammered piece of gold (Exod 25:31, 37:17). One of the earliest and most enduring Jewish religious symbols. After the Roman destruction of the Second Temple in 70 CE, the menorah taken from the Temple was paraded in Rome — depicted on the Arch of Titus, which still stands in the Forum. The image of that menorah on the Arch is one of the oldest visual representations of any Israelite cultic object that survives.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        9,
        1,
        "",
        "in the wilderness of Sinai, in the first month of the second year",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>A chronological seam.</strong> Verse 1 dates events to the first month of year two; chapter 1 verse 1 dates the census to the second month of year two. So Num 9 narratively precedes Num 1. Many readers and the rabbis have noticed this — the book of Numbers is not simply chronological. Possible explanations: thematic organization (cultic preparations 9, then census 1), or composite redaction stitching together earlier material. Either way, the displacement is intentional.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        15,
        3,
        "",
        "in your set feasts, to make a pleasant aroma",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Re’ach niḥo’aḥ.</strong> "Pleasant aroma" — literally "soothing scent." A widely attested ancient Near Eastern image of sacrifice as feeding the gods. The Hebrew Bible inherits the phrase but reshapes its theology — Yahweh does not need food (Ps 50:12–13), and the prophets later mock the idea (Isa 1:11). The phrase persists, however, retained as a poetic technicality even when its underlying mythology is rejected. Compare the demythologized survival of "wings of the wind" (Ps 18:10) and similar inherited imagery.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        18,
        20,
        "",
        "I  am your portion and your inheritance",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The priestly inheritance.</strong> The Levites and priests receive no land — Yahweh himself is their portion. The verse becomes a touchstone for Christian monastic and ministerial traditions (compare Ps 16:5, "Yahweh is my chosen portion"; Ps 73:26). The economic arrangement is significant: by holding no territory, the priestly class was structurally dependent on tithes and offerings, which kept them woven into the lay community rather than sealed off as a landed elite.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        20,
        1,
        "",
        "Miriam died there, and was buried there",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The death of Miriam.</strong> Miriam, Aaron, and Moses — the three siblings who led the exodus — die in this same chapter (Miriam 20:1, Aaron 20:28) and the next (Moses, foretold). The triplet death is a structural marker: the wilderness generation passes on. Rabbinic tradition associates Miriam with the well that traveled with Israel (cf. 1 Cor 10:4 in Paul) — when she dies, the water immediately stops (20:2), prompting the rock-striking incident.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        21,
        29,
        "",
        "Woe to you, Moab",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The taunt-song against Moab.</strong> Verses 27–30 are a quoted poetic fragment ("therefore those who speak in proverbs say"). Modern scholarship considers this song one of the earliest preserved Hebrew poems, possibly originating as a Moabite or Amorite victory song that Israel inverted and preserved. Jeremiah 48:45–46 quotes the same lines centuries later — a textual chain that gives a glimpse of how poetic material circulated and was reused across the prophetic tradition.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        23,
        7,
        "",
        "From Aram has Balak brought me",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The first Balaam oracle.</strong> Balaam’s four oracles (chs 23–24) are written in archaic Hebrew, with linguistic features that place them potentially in the early second millennium BCE — among the oldest poetic material in the Pentateuch. The oracles culminate in 24:17 ("a star shall come out of Jacob"), which became the central messianic proof-text of the Bar Kokhba revolt (132–135 CE). Bar Kokhba, "son of the star," was so named precisely from this verse.',
        "User original",
    ),
