    (
        31,
        2,
        "",
        "Avenge the children of Israel on the Midianites",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Midianite campaign.</strong> One of the most ethically difficult chapters in the Pentateuch — total war against Midian, including the killing of male children (31:17), the sparing of female virgins for distribution, and the meticulous division of plunder. Rabbinic and Christian commentary have struggled with this chapter from antiquity. Some readings frame it as judgment on a specific cult-led war (Baal-Peor); others read the text as honest portraiture of how ancient warfare actually was, which the Bible neither endorses nor sanitizes; others read it as ideological theology of the post-exilic compilers projected backward. The chapter resists comfortable resolution.",
        "User original",
    ),
