    (
        35,
        11,
        "",
        "cities of refuge",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The cities of refuge.</strong> The chapter institutes one of OT law’s most remarkable provisions — six designated cities (three on each side of the Jordan, Num 35:14) where a person who has killed someone unintentionally may flee from the blood-avenger (the <em>go’el ha-dam</em>) of the deceased. The institution preserves blood-vengeance as a recognized legal mechanism while creating a legal sanctuary that distinguishes intentional from unintentional killing — the OT’s clearest articulation of the criminal-law distinction we now call mens rea. The cities are listed in Josh 20:7–8 (Kedesh, Shechem, Hebron, Bezer, Ramoth-gilead, Golan), all of them Levitical cities, integrating cultic and judicial functions in the same spaces. Heb 6:18 takes the cities of refuge as theological figure for Christian salvation ("we who have fled for refuge"). The institution is one of the OT’s most institutionally creative innovations and has had long influence on the development of asylum law in Western legal systems.',
        "User original",
    ),
