    (
        35,
        25,
        "",
        "until the death of the high priest",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The amnesty of the high priest’s death.</strong> The killer who has fled to a city of refuge must remain there until the death of the current high priest, at which point he may return home and the blood-avenger may no longer pursue him. The provision is one of the OT’s strangest legal mechanisms: the high priest’s death functions as a kind of amnesty event that wipes the slate of unintentional bloodguilt for everyone currently in refuge. The theological logic, as developed by the rabbinic tradition (b. Makkot 11b) and Christian commentators (Heb 9:11–28 makes extensive Christological use of the parallel), treats the high priest’s death as cultically representative — his death "covers" the bloodguilt of those under refuge. The Letter to the Hebrews develops the analogy explicitly: Christ’s death functions as the eschatological high-priestly death that releases all who have fled for refuge to him. The verse is one of the OT’s richer examples of how a peculiar legal procedure becomes theological resource for later interpretive traditions.',
        "User original",
    ),
