    (
        36,
        6,
        "",
        "Let them be married to whom they think best",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Zelophehad daughters return.</strong> The chapter reopens the question first decided in Num 27 (where Zelophehad’s daughters won the right to inherit their father’s portion). The Manassite elders raise a complication: if the daughters marry outside the tribe, the inheritance leaves Manasseh permanently. The resolution: they may marry whomever they please — but only within the tribe of Manasseh. The chapter is the OT’s clearest case of a single inheritance-law working through successive cases over multiple chapters, with each iteration refining the principle: women may inherit (ch 27), women must marry within their tribe to preserve tribal patrimony (ch 36). The narrative arc — five named daughters as protagonists across two chapters of legal innovation — is one of the OT’s most extended treatments of female legal agency, even within the constraints the second ruling reimposes. Rabbinic tradition (b. Bava Batra 119b) celebrates the daughters as one of the great legal-precedent cases of Israelite jurisprudence.",
        "User original",
    ),
